| deity                 = Mizuwakasu-no-mikoto
| name                  = Mizuwakasu Shrine[[ja:水若酢神社]][[zh:水若酢神社]][[simple:Mizuwakasu_Shrine]]
}}
{{Expand Chinese|水若酢神社}}
{{Expand Japanese|水若酢神社}}
{{Interlanguage link|{{PAGENAME}}|simple||ja|水若酢神社|zh|水若酢神社}} is [[Shinto shrine|a Shinto shrine]] located in [[Oki District, Shimane|Oki District, Shimane]]. It is a [[Myojin Taisha|Myojin Taisha]]. It is also the [[Ichinomiya]] [[Oki Province]] or the highest ranked shrine in the province.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |title=Mizuwakasu Shrine {{!}} List of Geopark Sites and Treasures |url=https://www.oki-geopark.jp/en/geopark-sites-features-list/2694/ |access-date=2023-11-26 |website=Oki Islands UNESCO Global Geopark |language=en-US}}</ref> It is now a [[Beppyo shrine]] . The crest of the shrine has a chrysanthemum symbol".<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=2023-05-28 |title=水若酢神社（Mizuwakasu Shrine） - shrine-heritager |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230528184043/https://shrineheritager.com/en/%E6%B0%B4%E8%8B%A5%E9%85%A2%E7%A5%9E%E7%A4%BE%EF%BC%88mizuwakasu-shrine%EF%BC%89/ |access-date=2023-11-21 |website=web.archive.org}}</ref>

It enshrines the deity Mizuwakasu-no-mikoto. This deity is linked to land development in [[Oki Province]] and sea protection.<ref name=":1" /><ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=JSMvAAAAYAAJ&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&dq=Mizuwakasu&q=Mizuwakasu&hl=en |title=Shinto shrines and festivals |date=1958 |publisher=Association of Shinto Shrines, Kokugakuin University, Institute for Japanese Culture and Classics |language=ja}}</ref>

== History ==
The shrine's myth says the deity came from the sea near Igo. The deity then went over Mt. Ōmine. It settled in the old Goka Village.<ref name=":1" />

The shrine might date back to the Kofun Period. This was during Emperor Suijin or Emperor Nintoku's reign.<ref name=":1" />

In the [[Engishiki Jinmyocho]] it was listed as a [[Myojin Taisha]] in 927.<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=QmdRAAAAYAAJ&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&dq=Mizuwakasu&q=Mizuwakasu&hl=en |title=Engi-shiki; Procedures of the Engi Era: Books VI-X |date=1972 |publisher=Sophia University |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":1" />

The shrine is important in Oki Province. It guards the Sea of Japan. It appears in the Shoku Nihonki, a historical text. The Inshū Shicho Gakki, from the Edo Period, also mentions it. The Kokunai Jinmyōchō names it as Mizuwakasu Myojin Oki Ichinomiya Shrine.<ref name=":1" />

The present [[Honden]] was made in 1795<ref name=":0" />

It was severely damaged during [[Haibutsu kishaku]].<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Y5kSAQAAMAAJ&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&dq=Mizuwakasu&q=Mizuwakasu&hl=en |title=Business Japan |date=July 1988 |publisher=Nihon Kogyo Shimbun |language=en}}</ref>

== Architecture ==

=== Style ===
The shrine's style is unique. The front is Yuiitsu Shinmei-zukuri, like Ise Grand Shrine. The roof is Taisha-zukuri, like Izumo Grand Shrine. The pent roof is Kasuga-zukuri, like Kasuga Grand Shrine. This mix is called Oki-zukuri.<ref name=":1" />

=== Cultural Status ===
The shrine is an Important Cultural Property.<ref name=":1" />

== Festivals ==
The grand festival happens on May 3. It occurs in odd-numbered years.<ref name=":1" />

== See Also ==

* {{Ill|Yurahime Shrine|simple|4=ja|5=由良比女神社|6=zh|7=由良比女神社}} which also claims to be the [[Ichinomiya]] of [[Oki Province]]

