'''Mizuwakasu Shrine''' (水若酢神社, ''Mizuwakasu-jinja'') is a Japanese [[Shinto shrine]] located in [[Okinoshima, Shimane|Okinoshima]], [[Oki District, Shimane|Oki District]], [[Shimane Prefecture]]. It is a [[Myojin Taisha]]. It is also the [[Ichinomiya]] [[Oki Province]] or the highest ranked shrine in the province.<ref name="Oki-Islands-UNESCO-Global-Geopark-2023a">{{Cite web |title=Mizuwakasu Shrine {{!}} List of Geopark Sites and Treasures |url=https://www.oki-geopark.jp/en/geopark-sites-features-list/2694/ |access-date=2023-11-26 |website=Oki Islands UNESCO Global Geopark |language=en-US}}</ref> It is ranked as a [[Beppyo shrine]], or a special shrine by the [[Association of Shinto Shrines]]. The crest of the shrine has a chrysanthemum symbol.<ref name="水若酢神社-2023a">{{Cite web |date=2023-05-28 |title=水若酢神社（Mizuwakasu Shrine） - shrine-heritager |url=https://shrineheritager.com/en/%E6%B0%B4%E8%8B%A5%E9%85%A2%E7%A5%9E%E7%A4%BE%EF%BC%88mizuwakasu-shrine%EF%BC%89/ |access-date=2023-11-21 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230528184043/https://shrineheritager.com/en/%E6%B0%B4%E8%8B%A5%E9%85%A2%E7%A5%9E%E7%A4%BE%EF%BC%88mizuwakasu-shrine%EF%BC%89/ |archive-date=2023-05-28 }}</ref>

It enshrines the deity Mizuwakasu-no-mikoto. This deity is linked to land development in Oki Province and sea protection.<ref name="Oki-Islands-UNESCO-Global-Geopark-2023a" /><ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=JSMvAAAAYAAJ&q=Mizuwakasu |title=Shinto shrines and festivals |date=1958 |publisher=Association of Shinto Shrines, Kokugakuin University, Institute for Japanese Culture and Classics |language=ja}}</ref>

== History ==
According to legend, the deity rose from the sea, off the shore of Igo, crossed Mt. Ōmine and entered the former Goka Village.<ref name="Oki-Islands-UNESCO-Global-Geopark-2023a" />

The shrine might date back to the Kofun Period. This was during Emperor Suijin or Emperor Nintoku's reign.<ref name="Oki-Islands-UNESCO-Global-Geopark-2023a" />

In the [[Engishiki Jinmyocho]], it was listed as a [[Myojin Taisha]] in 927.<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=QmdRAAAAYAAJ&q=Mizuwakasu |title=Engi-shiki; Procedures of the Engi Era: Books VI-X |date=1972 |publisher=Sophia University |language=en}}</ref><ref name="Oki-Islands-UNESCO-Global-Geopark-2023a" />

The shrine is important in Oki Province. It guards the [[Sea of Japan]]. It appears in the [[Shoku Nihongi]], a historical text. The [[Inshū Shicho Gakki]], from the Edo Period, also mentions it. The {{ill|Owari Kokunai Jinmyōchō|sv}} names it as Mizuwakasu Myojin Oki Ichinomiya Shrine.<ref name="Oki-Islands-UNESCO-Global-Geopark-2023a" />

The present [[Honden]] was made in [[1795]].<ref name="水若酢神社-2023a" />

It was severely damaged during [[Haibutsu kishaku]].<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Y5kSAQAAMAAJ&q=Mizuwakasu |title=Business Japan |date=July 1988 |publisher=Nihon Kogyo Shimbun |language=en}}</ref>

== Architecture ==

=== Style ===
The shrine's style is unique. The front is Yuiitsu Shinmei-zukuri, like Ise Grand Shrine. The roof is Taisha-zukuri, like Izumo Grand Shrine. The pent roof is Kasuga-zukuri, like Kasuga Grand Shrine. This mix is called Oki-zukuri.<ref name="Oki-Islands-UNESCO-Global-Geopark-2023a" />

{{Infobox Chinese
| kanji = 水若酢神社
| romaji = Mizuwakasu jinja
| hiragana = みずわかすじんじゃ
}}

=== Cultural Status ===
The shrine is designated as an [[Important Cultural Property (Japan)|Important Cultural Property]] by the Japanese government.<ref name="Oki-Islands-UNESCO-Global-Geopark-2023a" />

== Festivals ==
The grand festival is held on May 3 in odd-numbered years.<ref name="Oki-Islands-UNESCO-Global-Geopark-2023a" />

